          "heading": "Experimental Laboratory Investigation: Interactive laboratory 10: Hydrodesulfurization residual sulfur",
          "content": "# Interactive laboratory 10: Hydrodesulfurization residual sulfur\n\n[Open lab in the browser](lab-10.html) \u00b7 [Unit 04](../units/unit-04.html)\n\n## Engineering purpose\n\nExplore an effective first-order sulfur concentration decline with increasing contact index. The selected input is intentionally a one-variable exploratory slice. Move one setting at a time, check whether the displayed rate or fraction remains physically meaningful, and compare the curve against the conservation requirements of the process. The exercise is designed to show sensitivity and falsifiability, not to choose equipment sizes or plant operating conditions.\n\n## Equations and units\n\n`Sout=Sin exp(\u2212k\u03c4)`\n\nThe result is reported as **ppmw**. Entered quantities are documented in the associated JSON and visible controls. Distinguish this calculated quantity from plant measurements and from a standardized certificate-of-analysis property. Whenever the formula contains a fitted coefficient or lumped index, describe what experimental dataset would be needed for calibration.\n\n## Procedure and recorded evidence\n\n1. Start from the displayed nominal controls and record the output with all units.\n2. Change the primary parameter to one end of its range. Use the plot to identify the sign and approximate local slope; record both the input and output.\n3. Return to nominal settings, vary the second parameter when available, and explain why the primary trend changes\u2014or why the one-dimensional model prevents a conclusion.\n4. Export the 81-point dataset. Plot it independently in a spreadsheet or numerical notebook; check the endpoint values by hand using the equation.\n5. Test a limiting or boundary case. State what would make the equation physically inappropriate or numerically invalid, even when the software shows a smooth graph.\n\n## Investigation\n\nFind contact index needed for 99% nominal removal at k=1/h. Compare your result with an independent arithmetic calculation. Report a mass-, species-, or energy-balance audit appropriate to the process rather than relying on numerical agreement alone.\n\n## Scientific boundary\n\nIgnores sulfur species, inhibition, deactivation and hydrodynamics. The graph is not evidence of a measured plant result. The model omits material characteristics, uncertainty sources and coupled constraints that would be required in a licensed process simulation. A useful lab report has separate sections for model prediction, measurement requirements, likely failure modes and decisions that must be deferred until validation."
